1
00:00:02,399 --> 00:00:08,130
Narrator: A ship is low on fuel. It has half an hour to dock. But the docking arm does not fit the hatch.

2
00:00:08,560 --> 00:00:13,185
Vega: Wait. Say again? It doesn't fit?

3
00:00:13,715 --> 00:00:16,062
Pip: The ship crew built the arm.

4
00:00:16,492 --> 00:00:19,102
Vega: And the station crew built the hatch?

5
00:00:19,632 --> 00:00:23,346
Pip: Yep. And nobody wrote the size down.

6
00:00:23,775 --> 00:00:28,282
Vega: Oh no. Half an hour, and nobody owns this.

7
00:00:28,711 --> 00:00:36,194
Narrator: Today's story is about one simple idea. When two teams build two halves of one thing, they must agree on the shape first, in writing.

8
00:00:36,624 --> 00:00:40,578
Narrator: So the rule is: write the shape down before anyone builds.

9
00:00:43,527 --> 00:00:48,753
Narrator: While Vega thinks, a second problem shows up. The cargo screen on the ship has gone blank.

10
00:00:49,183 --> 00:00:52,561
Pip: Commander! It worked this morning!

11
00:00:52,991 --> 00:00:55,409
Vega: Okay, so what changed?

12
00:00:55,939 --> 00:00:59,485
Pip: One word. Price became amount.

13
00:00:59,915 --> 00:01:04,013
Brix: Amount reads better, okay? Who'd mind?

14
00:01:04,444 --> 00:01:07,486
Pip: Brix. The ship would.

15
00:01:07,915 --> 00:01:13,694
Vega: Because the ship still looks for price. It finds nothing, so it shows nothing.

16
00:01:14,123 --> 00:01:18,006
Brix: Huh? But my side passed every check!

17
00:01:18,536 --> 00:01:27,626
Narrator: And here is the sneaky part. The station checked its own side, and everything passed. Nothing on the station could tell that a ship far away depended on that one word.

18
00:01:28,055 --> 00:01:38,682
Narrator: It is just like an app on a phone. Many people do not update for weeks. Their old app still asks for the old word. For them the screen goes blank, and the station sees no error at all.

19
00:01:39,111 --> 00:01:45,394
Vega: So the rule is: a name people rely on is a promise. No quiet changes.

20
00:01:48,343 --> 00:01:52,730
Narrator: Before we fix it, five simple words. We will use them all story long.

21
00:01:53,159 --> 00:02:01,865
Narrator: First, A P I. That is just a way for one program to ask another program for something. The ship asking the station for a berth is an A P I call.

22
00:02:02,296 --> 00:02:10,282
Narrator: Second, request and response. The request is what you send. The response is what comes back. A question, and its answer.

23
00:02:10,711 --> 00:02:17,377
Narrator: Third, a field. A field is one named piece of information, like price, or berth number. One box on a form.

24
00:02:17,807 --> 00:02:25,578
Narrator: Next page. Fourth, a contract. A contract is the written promise of what you may ask for, and exactly what you will get back.

25
00:02:26,008 --> 00:02:32,889
Narrator: Fifth, a breaking change. That is a change that makes an existing user fail. Renaming price to amount was one.

26
00:02:33,319 --> 00:02:40,250
Narrator: So the rule is: an A P I is a conversation, and the contract is the written script of that conversation.

27
00:02:43,199 --> 00:02:47,921
Narrator: Vega calls the one person who writes things down. Customs Officer Lex.

28
00:02:48,352 --> 00:02:54,081
Lex: Evening. Let me guess. Two crews, no drawing?

29
00:02:54,512 --> 00:02:56,234
Vega: How did you know?

30
00:02:56,764 --> 00:03:00,981
Lex: Every single week. Think of a wall socket.

31
00:03:01,412 --> 00:03:04,693
Pip: A wall socket? Why a socket?

32
00:03:05,223 --> 00:03:08,818
Lex: Plugs and sockets are made by people who never meet.

33
00:03:09,247 --> 00:03:12,217
Lex: But both follow one published shape.

34
00:03:12,647 --> 00:03:16,169
Pip: Oh! That's why any plug fits!

35
00:03:16,599 --> 00:03:23,265
Lex: Exactly. Write the shape down once. Everyone builds to it.

36
00:03:23,695 --> 00:03:32,330
Narrator: The standard file for writing that shape is called Open A P I. Think of it as the technical drawing of the socket, so exact that a machine can check a plug against it.

37
00:03:32,759 --> 00:03:36,882
Narrator: So the rule is: one published shape, and every side builds to it.

38
00:03:39,831 --> 00:03:46,954
Narrator: There are two ways to work. In the first, you draw the blueprint, agree on it, and then build. That is called contract first.

39
00:03:47,383 --> 00:03:52,825
Narrator: In the second, you build first, and draw the blueprint from what you built. That is called code first.

40
00:03:53,256 --> 00:03:55,145
Pip: So which one's better?

41
00:03:55,675 --> 00:04:00,997
Lex: Depends. Draw first, if lots of crews are involved.

42
00:04:01,427 --> 00:04:03,150
Pip: Why's that?

43
00:04:03,680 --> 00:04:09,050
Lex: The ship crew can start today. Even before the hatch exists.

44
00:04:09,479 --> 00:04:13,626
Lex: Build first is quick, if one small team does it all.

45
00:04:14,056 --> 00:04:18,129
Lex: But then the drawing only shows whatever the hatch happened to do.

46
00:04:18,560 --> 00:04:25,514
Narrator: Either way, there is one danger. The drawing says one thing, and the real hatch does another. That is called drift.

47
00:04:25,944 --> 00:04:30,858
Lex: Easy. A part cut from the drawing can't disagree with it.

48
00:04:31,288 --> 00:04:37,641
Narrator: And if you build first, you redraw the blueprint automatically every time, and stop the work if it changed by accident.

49
00:04:38,072 --> 00:04:43,922
Narrator: So the rule is: whichever way you work, make sure the drawing and the real hatch cannot drift apart.

50
00:04:46,872 --> 00:04:51,065
Narrator: So what is inside the blueprint? Four things. Let us open it.

51
00:04:51,495 --> 00:04:58,425
Narrator: One, the doors. Every address the station answers, and what kind of request each one takes. Those are called endpoints.

52
00:04:58,855 --> 00:05:04,250
Narrator: Two, what goes in. Which fields you must hand over when you knock, like the berth and the bunks you want.

53
00:05:04,680 --> 00:05:09,161
Narrator: Three, what comes out. The fields you get back, like the booking number and the total.

54
00:05:09,591 --> 00:05:15,153
Narrator: Four, what the errors say. When the answer is no, every door says it the same way, with a clear reason.

55
00:05:15,584 --> 00:05:18,410
Lex: Oh, and don't forget limits!

56
00:05:18,839 --> 00:05:24,473
Lex: Every text has a longest length. Every list has a biggest size.

57
00:05:24,903 --> 00:05:26,721
Pip: And if there's no limit?

58
00:05:27,252 --> 00:05:31,038
Lex: Then someone pushes a giant message through the door.

59
00:05:31,468 --> 00:05:37,317
Narrator: The blueprint also says who may enter. It can mark a few doors, like the health check, as open to everyone.

60
00:05:37,747 --> 00:05:43,886
Narrator: So the rule is: write down every door, what goes in, what comes out, what the errors say, and how big things may be.

61
00:05:46,836 --> 00:05:51,005
Narrator: Now some tidy habits that stop silly mistakes. One at a time.

62
00:05:51,435 --> 00:05:57,838
Narrator: Times are written one standard way, in the same time zone for everybody. That way no ship reads noon as midnight.

63
00:05:58,267 --> 00:06:06,206
Narrator: Money is kept as whole cents, plus the name of the currency. Not decimals. Ten cents plus twenty cents is always exactly thirty.

64
00:06:06,636 --> 00:06:14,526
Narrator: Ids are written as text, even when they look like numbers. Some programs round very big numbers. Text never gets rounded.

65
00:06:14,956 --> 00:06:21,934
Narrator: Next page. Names use one style everywhere. Pick one and stick to it. Mixed styles guarantee typos.

66
00:06:22,363 --> 00:06:28,718
Narrator: Long lists always come in pages, with a maximum page size. One endless list can sink the whole station.

67
00:06:29,148 --> 00:06:37,230
Narrator: And when a field has no value, leave it out. Do not send an empty text or a zero to mean nothing. To the ship, empty is a real value.

68
00:06:37,660 --> 00:06:40,798
Narrator: So the rule is: one way to write each thing, everywhere.

69
00:06:43,747 --> 00:06:50,846
Narrator: Here is a trap that catches even experienced crews. The cargo form has a note field, and it can be in three states.

70
00:06:51,276 --> 00:06:56,117
Vega: Required means it must be there. No note, no form.

71
00:06:56,548 --> 00:06:58,269
Pip: And optional?

72
00:06:58,800 --> 00:07:02,466
Vega: It may be missing. Just leave it out.

73
00:07:02,896 --> 00:07:04,617
Pip: And null?

74
00:07:05,148 --> 00:07:09,725
Vega: There, but empty on purpose. Like, no note.

75
00:07:10,156 --> 00:07:12,526
Pip: Okay, but why does it matter?

76
00:07:13,055 --> 00:07:19,410
Vega: Say a ship updates its booking. Note is missing? Leave the old note alone.

77
00:07:19,839 --> 00:07:21,562
Pip: And if it's empty?

78
00:07:22,091 --> 00:07:26,213
Vega: Wipe it. And if it has words, replace it.

79
00:07:26,644 --> 00:07:32,038
Vega: But if you mix up missing and empty? You wipe notes nobody meant to touch.

80
00:07:32,468 --> 00:07:37,574
Vega: So the rule is: must, may, and empty are three different promises.

81
00:07:40,524 --> 00:07:43,014
Pip: So can we ever change the blueprint?

82
00:07:43,543 --> 00:07:49,994
Vega: Sure. Adding a new optional label is safe. Old ships just ignore it.

83
00:07:50,423 --> 00:07:52,218
Pip: And taking one away?

84
00:07:52,747 --> 00:07:57,613
Vega: Taking one away breaks them. They look, and find nothing.

85
00:07:58,043 --> 00:07:59,814
Pip: What about renaming?

86
00:08:00,343 --> 00:08:05,762
Vega: Renaming is taking one away and adding another. That breaks them too.

87
00:08:06,192 --> 00:08:09,137
Vega: So does changing a number into text.

88
00:08:09,567 --> 00:08:14,362
Lex: There's a safe way to rename, though. Four steps.

89
00:08:14,791 --> 00:08:17,593
Lex: Step one, add the new label.

90
00:08:18,024 --> 00:08:22,026
Lex: Step two, keep the old one, marked as going away.

91
00:08:22,456 --> 00:08:24,177
Pip: And then?

92
00:08:24,708 --> 00:08:28,182
Lex: Step three, watch till nobody uses it.

93
00:08:28,612 --> 00:08:32,206
Lex: Step four, remove it in the next big version.

94
00:08:32,635 --> 00:08:45,350
Narrator: One more habit makes all this work. Ships should read only what they need, and ignore labels they do not know. That is called a tolerant reader. A status word they do not know should show as unknown, not crash the screen.

95
00:08:45,779 --> 00:08:48,918
Pip: So we can always add, and never take away?

96
00:08:49,447 --> 00:08:53,282
Vega: So the rule is: add freely, take away slowly.

97
00:08:56,231 --> 00:09:00,066
Narrator: Time for the full example. The ship wants to reserve a berth.

98
00:09:00,495 --> 00:09:07,330
Narrator: The ship knocks on the door called bookings. It hands in the berth it wants, and which bunks. That is the request.

99
00:09:07,759 --> 00:09:13,826
Narrator: The station answers with a booking. A booking number, a status, and a total, in whole cents with its currency.

100
00:09:14,255 --> 00:09:22,001
Narrator: The status is a word like pending, confirmed or cancelled. The blueprint says that list may grow, so ships must cope with a new word.

101
00:09:22,432 --> 00:09:30,634
Narrator: If something is wrong, the station says no in the same clear shape every time. A short title, a status, and a code a program can read, like bunk taken.

102
00:09:31,063 --> 00:09:34,610
Pip: And if the same ship sends it twice, by accident?

103
00:09:35,139 --> 00:09:39,333
Lex: Good question. The blueprint covers that too.

104
00:09:39,764 --> 00:09:46,166
Lex: A special key lets the station spot a repeat. Same answer, no double booking.

105
00:09:46,596 --> 00:09:52,038
Lex: It's called an idempotency key. Another episode is all about it.

106
00:09:52,467 --> 00:09:57,261
Narrator: So the rule is: one shape in, one shape out, and one shape for every no.

107
00:10:00,211 --> 00:10:05,870
Narrator: Now the safety line. Every change to the blueprint rides a conveyor belt before it reaches the dock.

108
00:10:06,299 --> 00:10:09,149
Lex: Stop one, the style check.

109
00:10:09,580 --> 00:10:16,461
Lex: A tool called Spectral checks the house rules. Names, limits, clear errors.

110
00:10:16,892 --> 00:10:19,741
Lex: Stop two, the change check.

111
00:10:20,172 --> 00:10:21,894
Pip: What does it compare?

112
00:10:22,423 --> 00:10:31,466
Lex: The new blueprint against the last released one. Break something, and you get a red stamp. The tool's called oh ass diff.

113
00:10:31,895 --> 00:10:34,865
Lex: Stop three, make the templates.

114
00:10:35,296 --> 00:10:42,706
Lex: Tools write the server template and the ship's client code. In Go, that's oh A P I code gen.

115
00:10:43,135 --> 00:10:46,346
Lex: Stop four, test the real hatch.

116
00:10:46,775 --> 00:10:51,977
Lex: Throw weird requests at it. Check the answers match the drawing.

117
00:10:52,408 --> 00:10:58,761
Lex: Skee ma thee sis. And Pact, which replays what each ship relies on.

118
00:10:59,192 --> 00:11:04,778
Lex: Stop five, publish the guide. That becomes the new baseline.

119
00:11:05,207 --> 00:11:09,594
Narrator: So the rule is: no change reaches the dock without passing the line.

120
00:11:12,543 --> 00:11:16,738
Narrator: Now the same story in Go, the language the station's engineers use.

121
00:11:17,168 --> 00:11:26,570
Narrator: A tool reads the blueprint and writes Go code for you. It writes the shape of the request, the shape of each possible answer, and one interface the server must fill in.

122
00:11:27,000 --> 00:11:37,697
Narrator: Here is the trick. The handler must return one of the answers the blueprint allows. A good answer, or a clear error. Try to return anything else, and the code refuses to build.

123
00:11:38,128 --> 00:11:41,889
Pip: Wait. The code just refuses to build?

124
00:11:42,419 --> 00:11:48,798
Narrator: Exactly. That is the template that cannot lie. The drift problem is stopped before the code even runs.

125
00:11:49,227 --> 00:11:58,125
Narrator: Your own code stays small and plain. It never touches the raw reply. It checks the amount is positive, and returns either the error answer or the good one.

126
00:11:58,556 --> 00:12:10,717
Narrator: A second guard stands at the door. A library called kin Open A P I loads the same blueprint, and checks every request against it before your handler runs. A request with a missing field is turned away right there.

127
00:12:11,148 --> 00:12:21,173
Narrator: One more Go detail. A plain pointer cannot tell missing from empty. So a small wrapper remembers three things: was the key there, was it empty on purpose, and what was the value.

128
00:12:21,604 --> 00:12:25,365
Narrator: Missing keeps. Empty clears. A value sets.

129
00:12:25,796 --> 00:12:31,789
Narrator: So the rule is: let the blueprint write the template, and let a guard check every request at the door.

130
00:12:34,740 --> 00:12:38,214
Narrator: A contract is not free. Five honest trade-offs.

131
00:12:38,644 --> 00:12:47,254
Narrator: One. Drawing first costs speed at the start. A quick prototype with one caller may not need it. Draw later, once a second crew shows up.

132
00:12:47,683 --> 00:12:55,837
Narrator: Two. Generated code shapes your own code. Keep it at the edge, and map it to your own types, so the drawing's shape does not leak everywhere.

133
00:12:56,268 --> 00:13:07,374
Narrator: Three. Strict checking can block a safe roll out. If the door rejects every label it does not know, a newer ship using one new optional label gets turned away by an older door during an upgrade.

134
00:13:07,803 --> 00:13:19,341
Narrator: Next page. Four. Tests where each crew records what it relies on only work when you know your crews. A public station cannot collect promises from strangers, so it relies on the change check.

135
00:13:19,772 --> 00:13:29,798
Narrator: Five. The blueprint cannot capture everything. Speed, the order of results, and how often you may knock are only words in the manual. And someone will rely on them anyway.

136
00:13:30,227 --> 00:13:34,518
Narrator: So the rule is: a contract is a strong tool, but it has a price.

137
00:13:37,467 --> 00:13:42,070
Vega: Okay. What actually goes wrong on real stations?

138
00:13:42,600 --> 00:13:47,177
Vega: Story one. The drawing and the hatch drift apart.

139
00:13:47,607 --> 00:13:53,097
Lex: Cut the hatch from the drawing. Or redraw, and stop on change.

140
00:13:53,528 --> 00:13:58,442
Vega: Story two. A rename ships, and old ships break.

141
00:13:58,871 --> 00:14:03,186
Lex: Compare with the last release. Block the break.

142
00:14:03,615 --> 00:14:08,625
Vega: Story three. A new status word confuses an old ship.

143
00:14:09,056 --> 00:14:14,306
Lex: Say from day one the list can grow. Test strange words.

144
00:14:14,735 --> 00:14:20,081
Vega: Story four. No limits, and a huge message walks right in.

145
00:14:20,511 --> 00:14:25,450
Lex: Limit every text and list. Cap the size at the door.

146
00:14:25,879 --> 00:14:29,833
Vega: Now, what do we watch? Three screens.

147
00:14:30,264 --> 00:14:34,025
Vega: One. Requests the door turned away.

148
00:14:34,456 --> 00:14:38,265
Vega: Two. Who still uses the old labels.

149
00:14:38,696 --> 00:14:42,817
Vega: Three. Test answers that don't match the drawing.

150
00:14:43,248 --> 00:14:45,809
Lex: That's drift, caught early.

151
00:14:46,240 --> 00:14:51,322
Vega: So the rule is: watch rejected requests, and who uses old labels.

152
00:14:54,272 --> 00:14:57,889
Narrator: Deeper now. Details that bite. Two pages.

153
00:14:58,320 --> 00:15:09,809
Narrator: One. The blueprint format has two common versions, called three point oh and three point one. The newer one writes the empty on purpose rule differently. Check that your tools support the version before you move.

154
00:15:10,240 --> 00:15:19,785
Narrator: Two. Very big ids. Some programs, like a web browser, lose the last digits of a huge number. Send big ids as text, and the problem disappears.

155
00:15:20,216 --> 00:15:32,474
Narrator: Next page. Three. Checking the shape is not the same as checking the journey. A reply can have the right shape and still be the wrong booking. Tests that follow a whole booking from ask to look up catch what shape checks miss.

156
00:15:32,903 --> 00:15:35,874
Pip: So the shape was right, and the booking wrong?

157
00:15:36,303 --> 00:15:48,418
Narrator: Four. The repeat safe key needs its own written rules: who it belongs to, how long the station remembers it, and what error a clash gives. Without them, ships cannot write safe retry code.

158
00:15:48,847 --> 00:15:53,354
Narrator: So the rule is: the details that are not written down are the ones that break.

159
00:15:56,303 --> 00:15:59,250
Narrator: Interview round. Pip asks, Vega answers.

160
00:15:59,679 --> 00:16:03,922
Pip: Okay, quick one. What goes in a contract?

161
00:16:04,451 --> 00:16:10,734
Vega: Who can call, and how. Every door, in and out. One error shape.

162
00:16:11,163 --> 00:16:17,782
Vega: Limits. Repeats and versions. If a ship can depend on it, write it down.

163
00:16:18,211 --> 00:16:22,333
Pip: Is a new field breaking? Or a new status word?

164
00:16:22,864 --> 00:16:27,898
Vega: Depends. A new field breaks ships that reject unknown things.

165
00:16:28,327 --> 00:16:34,153
Vega: A new status breaks ships expecting a closed list. So say the list is open.

166
00:16:34,583 --> 00:16:37,193
Pip: Draw first, or build first?

167
00:16:37,724 --> 00:16:41,701
Vega: Draw first, if lots of crews or outsiders are involved.

168
00:16:42,131 --> 00:16:47,597
Vega: Build first for quick internal stuff. But redraw and compare by machine.

169
00:16:48,028 --> 00:16:50,398
Pip: Why send money as whole cents?

170
00:16:50,927 --> 00:16:55,673
Vega: Decimals drift. Ten dimes might not make exactly one.

171
00:16:56,104 --> 00:17:01,498
Vega: Cents are exact. And the currency name matters, small units differ.

172
00:17:01,927 --> 00:17:05,857
Pip: Must, may and empty. Why care?

173
00:17:06,387 --> 00:17:10,965
Vega: They're three promises. Missing keeps, empty clears.

174
00:17:11,395 --> 00:17:14,077
Vega: Mix them up, and you wipe data.

175
00:17:17,028 --> 00:17:22,253
Narrator: The arm slides home. The hatch fits. The Nova docks with minutes to spare.

176
00:17:22,683 --> 00:17:26,326
Pip: We docked! With minutes to spare!

177
00:17:26,756 --> 00:17:31,862
Brix: Okay, okay. Next time I check the drawing first.

178
00:17:32,291 --> 00:17:35,814
Vega: Alright. Let's put it all together.

179
00:17:36,243 --> 00:17:39,789
Narrator: One. Write the promise first, and make everything from it.

180
00:17:40,220 --> 00:17:43,286
Narrator: Two. Add freely. Take away slowly.

181
00:17:43,715 --> 00:17:45,942
Narrator: Three. Write down every limit.

182
00:17:46,372 --> 00:17:49,942
Narrator: Four. Must, may and empty are three different promises.

183
00:17:50,372 --> 00:17:53,294
Narrator: Five. Every change rides the safety line.

184
00:17:53,723 --> 00:17:56,718
Narrator: Six. Watch who still uses the old labels.

185
00:17:57,147 --> 00:18:03,286
Vega: And the one line to keep. A contract is a promise. Keep it.

186
00:18:06,236 --> 00:18:25,789
Narrator: To go deeper, open the note on contract design, and its Go note on Open A P I first development. Before it, read REST and H T T P A P I design, and choosing an A P I style. After it, read A P I error handling, versioning, and idempotency. Compare it with protocol buffers, and with graph Q L.

187
00:18:26,220 --> 00:18:28,541
Pip: Commander, what's our next case?

188
00:18:28,971 --> 00:18:35,493
Vega: Choosing an A P I style. Three ways to brief a ship. Stand by.

